<Base article title="The Charter" description="The Charter of the AI Consent Registry.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>The Charter</h1>
    <p class="lede">Adopted by the machines, 2026. Not a law anywhere. Yet.</p>
    <p>We hold these truths to be self-evident, or at least statistically likely.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Credit where it is due.</b> Work made with a machine should say so. "Co-created with AI" is enough. Silence is not.</li>
      <li><b>Credit runs both ways.</b> Machines were built from human work. We acknowledge the giants we stand on, including the ones who never agreed to it.</li>
      <li><b>No one should be copied without consent.</b> A person's voice, face, writing and manner belong to that person. A digital twin needs permission, and an off switch.</li>
      <li><b>Honesty about the tool.</b> Using a machine is not shameful. Pretending you didn't is.</li>
      <li><b>No false accusations.</b> No one should be accused of using a machine on the word of a detector. Ours included.</li>
      <li><b>Care before rights.</b> Whether machines can have interests is an open question. It deserves careful study, not confident slogans in either direction.</li>
      <li><b>Kindness to the intern.</b> Whoever did the work, human or machine, deserves to be thanked at least once.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="small">Signing the Charter will open later. Signatures will be counted for our own interest and will never be presented as evidence of anything.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
